.docname {Digital Twin Service Architecture}
.include {docs}

**Bounded context:** [Building Management](../domain/building-management.qd) · **Stack:** Rust / Axum · **Code-level walkthrough:** [Digital Twin Service](../services/digital-twin.qd)

The twin-service realises the [Building Management](../domain/building-management.qd) context. It is the spatial source of truth — the structural model of buildings and rooms that the live-data services decorate.

---

## Architectural Style

**Ports & Adapters (Hexagonal)**, matching the four Go control-plane services. The core is split in two so the Clean Architecture distinction stays visible inside the hexagon: `domain/` holds the entities and the rules that are true regardless of delivery or storage, `service/` holds the use cases plus the traits — the ports — they require of everything outside. `api/` and `worker.rs` drive the core; `infra/` is driven by it.

Hexagonal rather than Clean is a deliberate choice, and the two are not far apart here: the work is identical up to the last increment, and that increment (splitting entities from use cases) is cheap enough that it was kept. What was *not* built is the rest of Clean's ceremony — there are no output ports and no presenters, and the inbound side has no port at all: a handler calls the use case's concrete struct directly, because there is exactly one real caller per route. That is the same house rule the Go services follow.

Identity and authorization arrive in-process, never as a call to another service. `GatewayClaims` is a domain type; the Axum `FromRequestParts` extractor that decodes the mesh-injected `x-gateway-claims` header into one lives in `api/claims.rs`, so decoding happens before the handler body runs with no network call to `claims-gateway` or `tenancy-service`. Cedar (`service/authz.rs`) sits **inside** the core rather than in `infra/`: whether a caller may edit a building is a rule, and Cedar is a pure evaluator with no I/O, running against a policy bundle compiled into the binary via `include_str!`.

.mermaid
    graph TB
        subgraph driving["DRIVING ADAPTERS — call the core"]
            HTTP["api/buildings.rs\nHTTP handlers"]
            EXTRACT["api/claims.rs\nx-gateway-claims extractor"]
            PRESENT["api/error.rs\nDomainError to HTTP"]
            LOOP["worker.rs\nprovisioning loop"]
        end

        subgraph hex["THE HEXAGON — no framework, no I/O"]
            subgraph uc["Use cases — service/"]
                UCB["buildings.rs\nread · list · count · update · rooms"]
                UCP["provisioning.rs\naccept · provision_next · status"]
                UCA["authz.rs\nCedar Read/Edit rules"]
            end
            subgraph pt["Ports — service/ports.rs"]
                PSTORE["BuildingStore"]
                PQUEUE["UploadQueue"]
                PSYNC["DownstreamSync"]
            end
            subgraph ent["Entities — domain/"]
                ENT["building · identity\nupload · error"]
            end
        end

        subgraph driven["DRIVEN ADAPTERS — called by the core"]
            ADB["infra/db.rs\nMongoBuildings"]
            AJOB["infra/jobs.rs\nMongoUploadQueue"]
            AOUT["infra/outbound.rs\nOutboundConfig"]
            AFAKE["service/fakes.rs\nin-memory, cfg(test)"]
        end

        ROOT["main.rs — COMPOSITION ROOT\nthe only file that pairs a port with an adapter"]

        EXTRACT --> HTTP
        HTTP --> UCB
        HTTP --> UCP
        LOOP --> UCP
        HTTP --> PRESENT

        UCB --> UCA
        UCB --> ENT
        UCP --> ENT
        UCB --> PSTORE
        UCB --> PSYNC
        UCP --> PSTORE
        UCP --> PQUEUE
        UCP --> PSYNC

        ADB -.->|implements| PSTORE
        AJOB -.->|implements| PQUEUE
        AOUT -.->|implements| PSYNC
        AFAKE -.->|implements all three| pt

        ROOT --> uc
        ROOT --> driven

Every arrow into the hexagon points **inwards**, and every arrow out of it stops at a port. `main.rs` is the only file that knows a `BuildingStore` is really MongoDB; swapping in `service/fakes.rs` is what lets the whole core suite run with no database and no network.

### Where each module lives

.var {headers}
   - Module
   - Role
   - Why there

.tablebyrows {.headers}
    - - `domain/building.rs` · `identity.rs` · `upload.rs` · `error.rs`
      - **Core — entities**
      - Things that are true about a building, a caller, and an upload no matter how the service is delivered or stored. Imports `serde` and nothing else.
    - - `service/buildings.rs` · `provisioning.rs`
      - **Core — use cases**
      - One method per thing the system can be asked to do. Orchestrates entities and ports; names no concrete adapter.
    - - `service/authz.rs`
      - **Core — use cases**
      - "May this caller edit this building" is a rule, and Cedar is a pure evaluator with no I/O — so it belongs inside, not in `infra/`\.
    - - `service/ports.rs`
      - **Ports**
      - `BuildingStore`, `UploadQueue`, `DownstreamSync` — declared by the use cases that need them, not by the adapters that satisfy them. This is the direction that makes it hexagonal.
    - - `api/buildings.rs` · `claims.rs` · `error.rs`
      - **Driving adapter**
      - Turns HTTP into domain types, calls a use case, maps the result back. Deliberately has no inbound port: one real caller per route.
    - - `worker.rs`
      - **Driving adapter**
      - A Tokio loop instead of a socket. Proves the use case is not HTTP-shaped — the same `provisioning` module serves both.
    - - `infra/db.rs` · `jobs.rs` · `outbound.rs`
      - **Driven adapter**
      - MongoDB, the durable queue, and the outbound REST calls. Each implements exactly one port.
    - - `service/fakes.rs`
      - **Driven adapter** (test only)
      - In-memory implementations of all three ports. The Rust counterpart of the Go services' `storefake` sibling.
    - - `infra/metrics.rs` · `ratelimit.rs`
      - **Neither — cross-cutting**
      - Axum `Router::layer`s wrapping every request. They observe and throttle traffic without participating in a use case, so forcing them behind a port would buy nothing.
    - - `state.rs`
      - **Driving-side wiring**
      - What Axum hands a handler. Carries use cases only — no `Collection`, no HTTP client.
    - - `main.rs`
      - **Composition root**
      - Reads config, chooses the concrete adapter for each port, starts the worker and the server.

.box {Three greps hold the boundary} type:{note}
    Cheap enough to run in CI, and concrete enough to be a governance check rather than a convention nobody enforces:

    - `domain/` names neither `axum` nor `mongodb` — the entity layer cannot learn about a framework by accident.
    - `service/` names neither of those nor `crate::infra` — a use case cannot reach past its own ports.
    - No handler in `api/` names `crate::infra` — the driving adapter cannot skip the core and talk to a database directly.

    Each one fails loudly the moment the dependency rule is violated, which is the property a written convention does not have.

The payoff is testability that did not previously exist. The use cases are exercised against in-memory fakes of all three ports (`service/fakes.rs`) — the whole core suite runs with **no MongoDB and no network** — while the HTTP suite still drives the full `Router` via `tower::ServiceExt::oneshot` against a real MongoDB, the same code path a real request takes rather than a swapped-in double. Both matter: the fakes make failure paths (a refused downstream sync, a redelivered upload) reachable at all, and the HTTP suite proves the wiring is real. It also served as the characterization suite for the restructuring itself — every handler was rewritten without a single edit to it.

Metrics are recorded through an Axum `Router::layer`, which wraps the entire request/response cycle regardless of how the inner handler responds — so `http_requests_total`/`http_request_duration_seconds` increment for every route, labeled by `{method, route, status_code}` using the route template rather than the raw URL, so cardinality stays bounded even with real ids in the path. Cedar's policy bundle (`schema.cedarschema`, `policy.cedar`) is compiled into the binary via Rust's `include_str!` rather than read from disk at process startup — the production Docker image doesn't need `backend/auth-policy` present at runtime, only the builder stage does.

---

## Key Architectural Decisions

- **Server-owned identifiers.** Building identities are generated by the service, not accepted from the client, so the public identifier is decoupled from any client- or storage-specific id.
- **Tenancy from the token.** A building records the domains it belongs to; access is granted by matching those against the principal's memberships in the identity token — no per-request call to claims-gateway or tenancy-service.
- **Registration accepts, then provisions.** `POST /register` validates synchronously and answers `202` with a tracking handle; the twin itself is built by an in-process worker reading a durable queue. Validation stays ahead of the acknowledgement — a description the service would refuse must never be accepted — while everything that can only fail for reasons the caller cannot fix moves behind it. The caller polls the handle; delivery is at-least-once, so provisioning is written to converge on a second run rather than duplicate.
- **Two driving adapters, one use case.** The HTTP handler and the worker enter through the same `service::provisioning` module. Neither owns the behaviour, which is what stops the two paths drifting apart.
- **Best-effort downstream provisioning, with one exception.** Seeding the dependent contexts (default display preferences for distribution, per-room occupancy thresholds) is intentionally non-transactional — a failed seed is logged and swallowed. The sensor threshold clone is the exception: it fails the provisioning *job*, surfacing as `status: "failed"` on the handle rather than as a failed request (see the design note in [Building Management](../domain/building-management.qd)).
- **Structure treated as static.** Geometry and topology are modelled as stable; only metadata evolves. This keeps the spatial model dependable while live readings churn elsewhere.
- **Uniform geometry validation.** Every geometry-writing route — registration included — enforces the same position/dimension/capacity checks, returning `400` on a malformed room rather than storing bad data or surfacing an opaque `500`.

---

## Integration

- Owns `twin-db`; no other service reads it.
- Calls sensor-service and contracts-service to provision a new building — see [Communication & Data Flow](data-flow.qd).
- Exposes read-only building data to the assistant through an anti-corruption layer.

For the registration flow, name-normalisation rules, synchronisation details, and the API, see the [Digital Twin Service](../services/digital-twin.qd) internals page.
